 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A fuel is a substance that burns in oxygen to release energy (heat and light).\nFossil fuels formed over millions of years from the remains of plants and animals buried under mud and rock, under heat and pressure:\n• coal: from ancient forests (a solid);\n• crude oil (petroleum): from tiny sea organisms (a liquid); it is refined into petrol, kerosene, diesel, gas and bitumen (SONARA refines oil at Limbe);\n• natural gas: mainly methane, found with oil (Cameroon produces gas off Kribi).\nFossil fuels are non-renewable: they are used up much faster than they form. Renewable sources (sun, wind, water, firewood if trees are replanted) can be replaced.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why is crude oil called a non-renewable fuel?",
  "steps": [
   "It took millions of years to form from buried sea organisms.",
   "We use it much faster than it can form, so it will run out."
  ],
  "answer": "It forms over millions of years but is used up quickly.",
  "checks": []
